$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The suit and the appeal</h2>
<p>A widow claimed she had married the appellant in 1997, cohabited with him until July 2010, and borne a daughter in March 2011. He denied the marriage altogether, asserting he had been married to another woman since 1999. Her petitions under the Hindu Marriage Act and Section 125 CrPC were dismissed in 2012 for failure to prove the marriage. The mother and daughter then sued for a direction for a DNA test and a declaration of paternity. The Family Court ordered the test on 31 August 2024; the alleged father appealed.</p>

<h2>Three objections, three answers</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>&ldquo;The 2012 findings bind them.&rdquo;</strong> They decided marriage, not parentage. The daughter&rsquo;s biological paternity was never adjudicated, and she cannot be shut out of her own claim by findings in proceedings between the adults.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>&ldquo;A DNA test is impermissible.&rdquo;</strong> The restraint courts exercise in matrimonial cases rests on the Section 112 legitimacy presumption and the collateral nature of paternity there. With no marriage proved, no presumption arises, and paternity is the suit&rsquo;s central issue — precisely the situation where the test is appropriate. Precedent urging restraint in a divorce-adultery setting was distinguished on that basis.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>&ldquo;Draw an adverse inference instead.&rdquo;</strong> Following Rohit Shekhar v. Narayan Dutt Tiwari, an adverse inference from non-compliance cannot replace an enforceable direction. In the Court&rsquo;s words, <em>&ldquo;adverse inference from non-compliance cannot be a substitute to the enforceability of a direction for DNA testing.&rdquo;</em></div>
</div>

<h2>Privacy against identity</h2>
<p>The heart of the judgment is its balancing exercise. The appellant invoked privacy and the risk of defamation; the child invoked her right to know who her father is. The Bench held that <em>&ldquo;the right of the appellant to privacy has to be balanced against the right of the respondent no.2 to know her origin,&rdquo;</em> adding that <em>&ldquo;justice is not served by impeding the establishment of truth.&rdquo;</em> Privacy was protected procedurally rather than by denying the test: samples are to be collected at the Forensic Medicine department of BSA Hospital, and the report goes to the Family Court in a sealed cover.</p>

<h2>Where DNA directions now stand</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Approach of the courts</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Child born during a proved marriage</td><td>Section 112 presumption of legitimacy applies; a DNA test is ordered only in exceptional cases, since it risks bastardising the child</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Paternity collateral to another dispute (e.g. adultery in divorce)</td><td>Strong restraint; a roving test is refused where the issue can be decided on other evidence</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No marriage proved; paternity is the central issue</td><td>The test may be directed with confidentiality safeguards, because the suit cannot be fairly decided without it — the situation in this case</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">Directions for scientific testing remain discretionary and fact-sensitive. This decision does not make DNA tests available on demand; it confirms that where parentage itself is the lis and no statutory presumption operates, a court may compel the truth to be established, shielding the parties through sealed-cover and institutional safeguards.</div>
